    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A privacy fence is one of the most effective ways to turn an overlooked garden into a secluded outdoor room. Beyond screening you from neighbours and passers-by, a well-built fence helps keep children and pets in, can soften some traffic noise and gives planting a clean backdrop. The price, though, varies a great deal from one garden to the next, depending on the materials, the labour and the site itself.</p>
<p>Fencing prices also change by region, supplier and season, so rather than quote figures that will soon be out of date, this guide explains what makes one fence cost more than another. Once you understand the drivers, you can read quotes with confidence and decide where it is worth spending and where it is not.</p>

<h2 id="material">1. Material</h2>
<p>The material is usually the biggest single factor in the price, and it also decides how much upkeep the fence will need for years to come.</p>
<h3>Pressure-treated softwood</h3>
<p>Pressure-treated softwood, typically pine, spruce or fir treated with preservative, is usually the cheapest option and the most widely available. It suits tight budgets and DIY projects. The trade-off is maintenance: it needs re-treating or staining at the interval the finish manufacturer recommends, and cheaper timber is more prone to warping, splitting and shrinking.</p>
<h3>Cedar and larch</h3>
<p>Cedar and larch cost noticeably more than treated softwood. Their natural oils help them resist decay, they tend to be more stable, and they have a warmer, more refined look. Left untreated, both weather to a silver-grey; a periodic oil keeps the original colour. In the US, redwood is another premium choice in the same bracket.</p>
<h3>Vinyl (PVC)</h3>
<p>Vinyl, or PVC, fencing costs more than softwood to buy but needs no staining, painting or sealing, only an occasional wash. Over the life of the fence, the maintenance you avoid can close much of the gap. Quality matters: cheaper vinyl can become brittle in hard frosts or fade in strong sun, so ask about UV protection and the profile thickness.</p>
<h3>Composite</h3>
<p>Composite fencing, made from wood fibres and recycled plastics, sits towards the top of the range. It does not rot or splinter, resists fading and needs very little upkeep, and the boards are heavy and solid. The heavier panels can need sturdier posts, which adds to the cost.</p>
<h3>Metal panels</h3>
<p>Solid aluminium or steel panels, and corrugated metal for an industrial look, suit contemporary gardens. Aluminium is light and does not rust, which makes it a good choice in damp or coastal gardens. Steel is strong but needs a good coating, and cut edges of corrugated sheet must be finished or capped so they are safe. Metal systems generally sit in the mid-to-upper part of the range, with bespoke designs costing more.</p>

<h2 id="style">2. Style</h2>
<p>Within any one material, the style of fence changes how much material and labour it takes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Closeboard (featheredge):</strong> overlapping vertical featheredge boards fixed to horizontal rails between posts. A traditional, sturdy UK choice; built on site, so it uses more labour than panels.</li>
<li><strong>Ready-made panels:</strong> overlap or lap panels slotted between posts are usually the quickest and cheapest to install, though often less robust in exposed gardens.</li>
<li><strong>Hit-and-miss (shadowbox):</strong> boards fixed alternately on each side of the rails, so the fence looks good from both sides and lets wind pass through. It uses more boards than a single-sided fence.</li>
<li><strong>Board-on-board:</strong> overlapping boards that keep full privacy even as timber shrinks. It needs more timber again, so it costs more.</li>
<li><strong>Slatted (horizontal or vertical):</strong> a contemporary look with gaps between slats. It needs accurate spacing and straight timber, so materials and labour both cost more than a basic panel fence.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="height">3. Height</h2>
<p>Height affects cost more than many people expect. A taller fence needs more material, but it also needs longer posts set in deeper footings, because a solid fence acts like a sail and catches more wind. Taller fences may need closer post spacing or heavier posts, and the work takes longer.</p>
<p>Height is also where the rules come in. In England, a fence over 2 m high, or over 1 m next to a highway used by vehicles, usually needs planning permission, and stricter rules can apply to listed buildings, conservation areas and some newer estates. In the US, height limits vary by city and county, front and back gardens are often treated differently, and some places ask for engineered drawings for taller fences. Check with your local planning authority or building department, and any homeowners' association (HOA), before you settle on a height.</p>

<h2 id="posts">4. Posts and gravel boards</h2>
<p>Posts carry the whole fence, so they are not the place to cut corners. In the UK, the main choice is between timber posts and concrete posts. Concrete posts usually cost more and are heavier to handle, but they do not rot and are less likely to fail in wind; slotted concrete posts also make replacing a panel later much easier. Gravel boards, in concrete or treated timber, sit at the base of the fence to keep the boards or panels off damp soil, and they add to the material list.</p>
<p>Post spacing is commonly around 1.8–2.4 m (6–8 ft), matching the panel width, but follow the fence supplier's guidance for your system and height. Wider spacing can let panels sag or fail in high winds; closer spacing adds posts and labour.</p>

<h2 id="labour-and-access">5. Labour and access</h2>
<p>Labour is a significant part of any fencing job, and how much depends on local rates, the style of fence and the site. A straight run of panels on flat, clear ground is relatively quick. Several things add time:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Access:</strong> if the only way into the back garden is through the house or a narrow side gate, posts, panels, concrete and spoil all have to be carried by hand.</li>
<li><strong>Removing the old fence:</strong> taking down an old fence, digging out old post footings and disposing of the waste all add labour and possibly a skip.</li>
<li><strong>Hedges and planting:</strong> clearing overgrown shrubs or protecting borders along the fence line takes extra care.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="terrain">6. Terrain and soil</h2>
<p>The ground itself is a hidden cost driver. On a slope, the fence is either stepped, with each panel set level and dropping down the hill, or racked, with the rails following the slope. Both take more time and precision than a level run, and stepped fences leave triangular gaps at the base that need gravel boards or infill.</p>
<p>Soil matters too. Digging post holes in soft loam is fast; rocky ground, heavy clay or old tree roots slow everything down and may need a power auger or breaker. Roots near trees need care, because cutting large roots can harm the tree; ask your installer, and check for tree preservation orders in the UK. Always check for buried cables and pipes before digging: use utility plans or a cable avoidance tool in the UK, and call 811 in the US.</p>

<h2 id="gates-and-hardware">7. Gates and hardware</h2>
<p>Each gate adds noticeably to the price. A gate needs stronger posts, hinges, a latch and often a lock, and a wide or double gate for bins, bikes or a car needs heavier posts and hardware again. Matching a gate to a slatted or composite fence can mean a bespoke frame. Fixings matter too: galvanised or stainless steel nails, screws and brackets cost more than plain steel but will not stain the timber or rust through.</p>

<h2 id="permits-and-boundaries">8. Permissions, HOA rules and boundaries</h2>
<p>Before any posts go in, check what permission you need. As well as planning rules on height, some areas restrict materials or colours, and in the US many HOAs set rules on fence height, style and colour; building without approval can mean having to change or remove the fence. Ask about any permit fees when you contact your local authority.</p>
<p>Be certain where your boundary is. Check your title plan or property deeds, talk to your neighbour, and if the line is unclear or disputed, consider a land surveyor before you build. Putting a fence on the wrong side of the line is a costly mistake to undo.</p>

<h2 id="comparing-quotes">How to get fence quotes you can compare</h2>
<p>Fence quotes are only comparable if they price the same job. Give two or three installers the same measurements and specification, and ask for an itemised quote covering:</p>
<ul>
<li>The total length of fence and its height, including any stepped or racked sections.</li>
<li>The material and style, including board or panel type, thickness and finish.</li>
<li>The post type (timber or concrete), size, spacing and how posts are set.</li>
<li>Gravel boards and capping or trellis, if included.</li>
<li>Each gate, with its size, frame, hinges, latch and lock.</li>
<li>The grade of fixings and hardware.</li>
<li>Removal and disposal of the old fence and footings.</li>
<li>Site preparation: clearing, digging in difficult ground and access arrangements.</li>
<li>Any permits, approvals or drawings, and who arranges them.</li>
<li>Any guarantee on workmanship, and the manufacturer's warranty on materials.</li>
</ul>
<p>If one quote is much lower than the rest, check which of these items it leaves out.</p>

<h2 id="ways-to-save">Ways to keep the cost down</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep the height to what you actually need for privacy; a trellis topper or tall planting can add screening where you need it most.</li>
<li>Use the premium material only where it is seen, for example slatted cedar along the patio and plain closeboard down the side of the garden.</li>
<li>Choose ready-made panels and standard sizes rather than a bespoke design.</li>
<li>Reuse sound posts only if an installer confirms they are in good condition and properly set.</li>
<li>Limit the number of gates, and keep wide gates to where you really need them.</li>
<li>If you share a boundary, agree with your neighbour to share the cost of a new fence, and put the agreement in writing, including who owns and maintains it.</li>
<li>Book outside the busiest season, when installers may have more flexibility.</li>
</ul>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Ask installers to price both timber and concrete posts in the same quote. It is the clearest way to see what the longer-lasting option adds, and you can weigh it against the cost of replacing rotten posts later.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Does a privacy fence add value to a home?</h3>
<p>A well-kept fence can make a garden feel finished and private, which may add appeal for buyers, especially those with children or pets. Whether it adds value depends on your local market and the condition of the property as a whole, so think of it mainly as an investment in how you use your garden.</p>
<h3>How long does a privacy fence last?</h3>
<p>It depends on the material, your climate, how the posts are set and how well the fence is maintained. Cedar, larch, vinyl, composite and metal typically outlast basic treated softwood, and concrete posts avoid the rot that ends the life of many timber fences. Check the manufacturer's warranty for its stated term.</p>
<h3>Is it cheaper to build a fence yourself?</h3>
<p>You save on labour, but not always as much as you might hope. You will need to hire or buy tools such as a post-hole digger or auger, handle heavy posts and concrete, and dispose of the old fence. Mistakes in setting posts lead to a leaning fence that is expensive to put right. A short, straight run of panels on level ground is a reasonable DIY project; tall fences, slopes, rocky ground and gates are usually worth leaving to a professional.</p>
<h3>What is the most durable material for a privacy fence?</h3>
<p>Composite, good-quality vinyl and powder-coated aluminium resist rot, insects and moisture, and need little upkeep. Among timbers, cedar and larch hold up better than standard softwood. Whatever you choose, sound posts and good drainage at the base make the biggest difference.</p>
<h3>How far apart should fence posts be?</h3>
<p>Commonly around 1.8–2.4 m (6–8 ft), matching the panel width, but follow the fence supplier's guidance for your system, height and how exposed the garden is.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>The cost of a privacy fence comes down to the material and style, the height, the posts, the labour and access, the ground and the gates, plus what it takes to meet local rules. An itemised quote based on the same specification is the clearest way to see where your money goes. Before you commit, read up on the common <a href="/blog/privacy-fence-mistakes">privacy fence mistakes</a>, and if you are planning the rest of the garden, see our guides to <a href="/blog/landscape-edging-pros-and-cons">landscape edging pros and cons</a> and <a href="/blog/deck-railing-cost">deck railing cost</a>. You can also explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service, or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a> to plan your outdoor space with a designer.</p>
